5. rescan the **entire** remediation registry, not merely the current tier or neighboring defects;
6. select the highest-priority unrelated `ACTIONABLE` correction under severity + dependency + current-source evidence;
7. continue implementation.

The exhaustive rescan in step 5 includes at minimum:

- Tier/current-priority items in Section 16.1;
- historical P0/P1 reconsideration in Section 16.2;
- all dependency-eligible legacy F3–F22 entries in Section 10 / Appendix A;
- open review-fix work discovered on `review/remediation`;
- evidence-preservation or source-audit work that can materially advance an otherwise evidence-blocked defect without inventing a root cause.

A defect that needs a physical device, unavailable synchronized evidence, or an external product decision is skipped temporarily unless there is still a safe source/test/evidence-preservation action that materially advances it. The blocker is defect-local; it does not freeze the project.

The run may stop for **lack of actionable work** only after an exhaustive current-source queue scan establishes that every remaining item is independently closed, dependency-blocked, evidence-blocked with no presently possible evidence-gathering action, product-decision-blocked, explicitly deferred, `NO_CHANGE_REQUIRED_CURRENT_HEAD`, or `NOT_APPLICABLE_CURRENT_HEAD`. Resource exhaustion and inability to leave a coherent repository state remain separate legitimate stop conditions under Section 11.7.

## 11.3 One defect at a time

Do not keep several unrelated working-tree corrections open simultaneously.

Before switching to another defect, the current correction should be in one of these states:

- pushed `CANDIDATE_COMPLETE`;
- explicitly deferred/blocked with the working tree restored to a coherent state;
- `NO_CHANGE_REQUIRED_CURRENT_HEAD` or `NOT_APPLICABLE_CURRENT_HEAD` with the exact current-source basis recorded;
- independently reviewed and followed up as required.

This rule keeps later independent review attributable even when the branch advances far beyond an earlier defect.

## 11.4 Self-review after implementation

Self-review is mandatory before moving on.

The implementation model must:

- re-open the current production files rather than trusting its patch intent;
- inspect all current callers/consumers of changed APIs;
- search for stale writers/bypass paths;
- trace retry/recovery/process-death paths;
- inspect destructive consumers;
- verify tests actually cross the intended production boundary;
- verify the fix did not merely make the test fixture agree with the bug;
- inspect the entire defect domain, not only the added lines.

If self-review finds a blocker before commit, fix it before the candidate commit when cohesive.

If self-review finds a blocker after the first candidate commit/push, make a new additive child commit. Never amend.

Self-review status may be `PASS_NO_KNOWN_BLOCKERS`, but never `CLEAN`.

## 11.5 External-review priority policy

`review/remediation` is the authoritative repository inbox for independent review records. The implementation model reads it; it never authors independent verdicts there.

When a new independent review record becomes visible, prioritize it as follows:

1. independent-review P0 blocker;
2. independent-review P1 blocker;
3. bring the current in-progress working tree to the nearest safe coherent commit boundary when switching immediately would leave partial semantics;
4. independent-review P2 blocker according to dependency and risk;
5. next highest-priority actionable master-plan defect.

A newly established `CLEAN` record may unlock a previously blocked dependency, but only after the **ancestor-CLEAN carry-forward guard** below is satisfied against the then-current implementation HEAD.

### 11.5.1 Ancestor-CLEAN carry-forward guard

An independent `CLEAN` record proves the reviewed defect domain at the exact reviewed implementation SHA. It does not automatically prove every later implementation HEAD.

Before using a `CLEAN` record for an ancestor SHA to unlock a dependency on a later current HEAD:

1. identify the exact reviewed implementation SHA and the current implementation HEAD;
2. inspect every intervening semantic commit between them;
3. determine whether any intervening commit touches, calls into, rewires, persists, consumes, deletes, recovers, retries, or otherwise semantically affects the reviewed defect domain or one of its established invariants;
4. if the intervening commits are demonstrably unrelated to that domain, the established CLEAN may carry forward until concrete regression evidence appears;
5. if any intervening commit touches or may semantically affect the reviewed domain, do **not** infer carry-forward CLEAN merely because the older checkpoint was CLEAN;
6. run focused regression evidence against the later candidate and require a new independent review of the later candidate whenever semantic preservation is not independently obvious from the intervening history;
7. never use a self-review, implementation-agent assertion, absence of a new review file, or unchanged test name as a substitute for this guard.

Attribution is irrelevant to this protection: even an unrelated-defect commit can invalidate carry-forward if it changes shared authority or lifecycle code used by the reviewed domain.

When the guard succeeds, re-evaluate the queue immediately. For example:

```text
BUG-OUTPUT CLEAN appears
    → inspect intervening commits through current HEAD
    → if BUG-OUTPUT domain is untouched, Finding B becomes actionable
    → if shared/output authority changed, require current-candidate evidence/review first

Finding B CLEAN appears
    → inspect intervening commits through current HEAD
    → if the Finding-B/F1 domain is preserved, mandatory full F1 close-out becomes actionable
```

A `NOT_CLEAN` record for an older candidate remains actionable even if the implementation branch has advanced. Remediate it with a new additive commit on the latest implementation HEAD after checking that intervening commits have not already changed the relevant semantics.

If an independent P0 arrives while another correction is in progress, do not leave the tree syntactically/semantically broken. Bring the current working tree to the nearest safe coherent boundary, then switch immediately.

Rebase/amend/squash/force-push are unnecessary and forbidden for remediation history.

## 11.6 Review-fix commit attribution

A later review-fix commit must identify:

- `Defect-ID`;
- `Reviewed-Checkpoint` SHA;
- independent finding ID/title if one exists;
- severity;
- semantic invariant repaired.

Recommended commit-body trailers or equivalent report fields:

```text
Defect-ID: BUG-OUTPUT-01
Reviewed-Checkpoint: <sha>
Review-Finding: OUTPUT-FOLLOWUP-XX
Review-Severity: P2
```

The exact trailer names need not become a repository-wide schema unless current project conventions already define one, but equivalent information must be recoverable from the report/record.

## 11.7 Resource-exhaustion stop rule

A long Ultra run should use available capacity aggressively, but must not sacrifice repository integrity at the end. Do not use this section as a generic early-stop escape hatch: lack-of-actionable-work termination is governed by the exhaustive proof in Section 11.2.1.

If resources become constrained:

- finish the current correction only if it can be brought to a tested coherent candidate;
- otherwise restore/leave the implementation branch at the last pushed coherent candidate rather than pushing known-broken partial semantics;
- report the unstarted/unfinished next defect and exact source findings discovered so far;
- never fabricate test execution to make a partial correction look complete.

---

# 12. Plan, implementation, review, and ledger branch interaction

The project now uses four explicit remote histories with different authority.

## 12.1 Branch roles

### `plan/remediation`

Contains the versioned authoritative Master Plan and planning/governance revisions only. The canonical plan text is the exact concatenation, in lexical filename order, of every file under:

`remediation/master-plan/parts/`

The manifest is:

`remediation/master-plan/README.md`

Canonical read command:

```bash
PLAN_SHA=<resolved-plan-sha>
for p in $(git ls-tree -r --name-only "$PLAN_SHA" remediation/master-plan/parts | sort); do
  git show "$PLAN_SHA:$p"
done
```

The part representation exists only to keep each repository write small and verifiable. Part boundaries have no semantic meaning.

The implementation runner may fetch/read this branch but must not modify it during ordinary remediation work. At run bootstrap, the runner pins the exact Plan SHA and uses that governance snapshot for the run. A later plan commit is adopted only on explicit user instruction or a controlled migration rule in the pinned plan. Plan commits do not create independent CLEAN verdicts and do not modify production semantics.

### `checkpoint/pre-baseline-review`

Contains semantic production/test corrections. Implementation models write here using additive commits only.

### `review/remediation`

Official independent-review history. This branch was created from the closure-ledger anchor `899328bc91e4008e39a658387396a0106c8666ec` on 2026-09-03 and bootstrapped by `4c7be32beecb6a954b96b8d61385d11740f5e641` (`review: initialize independent review inbox`) adding `reviews/README.md`. The previously established independent `BUG-OUTPUT-01 — NOT_CLEAN` review for implementation SHA `0a00e35af07632aa6b40a8cc5f7c003b33d73c56` was then backfilled additively at review commit `d8ac4865bf06f8778c76abede58b2a9f7e616ae3` in `reviews/BUG-OUTPUT-01/0a00e35af07632aa6b40a8cc5f7c003b33d73c56-review.md`. Review commits after the creation anchor are independent metadata/evidence history and do not need to be ancestors of implementation commits.

Recommended layout:

```text
reviews/
  BUG-OUTPUT-01/
    <reviewed-implementation-sha>-review.md
  FINDING-B/
    <reviewed-implementation-sha>-review.md
  CURRENT-PLAYER-TIMELINE-INDEX/
    <reviewed-implementation-sha>-review.md
  ...
```

Only an independent reviewer may author semantic verdicts on this branch. The implementation model may fetch/read it but must not create, modify, rewrite, or delete independent-review records on the reviewer’s behalf.

Review history is additive:

- no amend/rebase/squash/force-push;
- a newer review of a later candidate gets a new record/commit;
- if a prior review contained a factual correction, preserve the old record and add a new correcting record rather than rewriting history.

### `ledger/remediation`

Contains independently established closure/metadata records only. It is not a scratch review queue and must not be modified by the implementation model during normal defect work.

## 12.2 Review record schema

Every independent review record should identify at minimum:

- `Defect-ID`;
- `Reviewed-Implementation-SHA`;
